The nettle backend of util/random.c seeds yarrow from getentropy(3), which
does not work on Apple platforms:

    util/random.c:192:5: error: call to undeclared function 'getentropy'
    util/random.c:76:10: fatal error: 'sys/random.h' file not found

Darwin declares getentropy in <sys/random.h> rather than <unistd.h>, and
unbound declares it itself only where it also supplies an implementation --
which excludes Darwin, because it has arc4random.  <sys/random.h> in turn
ships only in the macOS SDK: it is in neither the iPhoneOS nor the
iPhoneSimulator SDK, and Apple treats getentropy as private API outside
macOS, so using it gets an app rejected from the App Store however the
declaration is obtained.

arc4random_buf(3) is public API on every Apple platform, declared in
<stdlib.h> (which config.h already includes), drawn from the same system
entropy, and cannot fail -- so the error branch goes with it.  unbound
already seeds itself this way in its non-nettle configurations.

The combination is untested upstream: an openssl or libbsd build takes the
arc4random branch of this file and never reaches the nettle one.

--- a/util/random.c
+++ b/util/random.c
@@ -189,6 +189,14 @@
 	/* Setup Yarrow context */
 	yarrow256_init(&s->ctx, 0, NULL);
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+	/* Darwin declares getentropy(3) in <sys/random.h>, which ships only in the macOS SDK, and
+	 * Apple treats the call itself as private API on its other platforms.  arc4random_buf(3) is
+	 * public everywhere, cannot fail, and is seeded from the same source. */
+	arc4random_buf(buf, sizeof(buf));
+	yarrow256_seed(&s->ctx, YARROW256_SEED_FILE_SIZE, buf);
+	s->seeded = yarrow256_is_seeded(&s->ctx);
+#else
 	if(getentropy(buf, sizeof(buf)) != -1) {
 		/* got entropy */
 		yarrow256_seed(&s->ctx, YARROW256_SEED_FILE_SIZE, buf);
@@ -199,6 +207,7 @@
 		free(s);
 		return NULL;
 	}
+#endif
 
 	return s;
 }
